{
  "id": "course-schedule-ii",
  "questionId": "course-schedule-ii",
  "slug": "course-schedule-ii",
  "title": "Course Schedule II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Topological sort",
  "patterns": [
    "topological sort",
    "graph",
    "kahn's algorithm"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "this is course-schedule, but now you need the actual order, and it must be deterministic - what rule pins down one specific valid order among many?",
    "at each step there may be several courses with zero remaining prerequisites - always take the smallest id among them",
    "a min-heap of available (in-degree 0) course ids makes 'always pick the smallest' efficient",
    "pop the smallest id, add it to the result, decrement the in-degree of everything that depended on it, and push any course whose in-degree just hit zero",
    "if the result doesn't contain all numCourses courses at the end, a cycle exists - return an empty array"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a plain FIFO queue instead of a min-heap, which does not guarantee the smallest-id tie-break and produces a non-canonical order",
    "returning a partial order instead of an empty array when a cycle is detected",
    "forgetting to include a course that has zero prerequisites from the very start"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There are `numCourses` courses, labeled 0 to numCourses - 1. You're given\na list of `prerequisites` pairs, where each pair [a, b] means you must\ncomplete course b before you can take course a.\n\n**Return a valid order in which to take all the courses.**\n\nSince more than one valid order can exist, make it deterministic: run Kahn's algorithm and, whenever more than one course is available to take next, always pick the one with the smallest course number. If it's impossible to finish all courses (a cycle exists), return an empty array.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= numCourses <= 10^4",
      "0 <= prerequisites.length <= 5000",
      "prerequisites[i] = [a, b] with 0 <= a, b < numCourses"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a cycle exists somewhere in the graph - return an empty array",
      "numCourses = 1 with no prerequisites",
      "no prerequisite pairs at all - result is simply ascending order",
      "a diamond dependency where two courses converge into a shared successor",
      "a self-loop prerequisite, which is itself a cycle"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "numCourses = 4, prerequisites = [[1, 0], [2, 0], [3, 1], [3, 2]]",
        "output": "[0, 1, 2, 3]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 1, 2, 3]",
        "explanation": "0 is the only course available first; after it, both 1 and 2 become available and the smaller id 1 is taken first"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "numCourses = 2, prerequisites = [[1, 0], [0, 1]]",
        "output": "[]",
        "expectedOutput": "[]",
        "explanation": "course 0 needs course 1 and course 1 needs course 0 - a cycle, so no valid order exists"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "numCourses = 3, prerequisites = []",
        "output": "[0, 1, 2]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 1, 2]",
        "explanation": "with no prerequisites at all, every course is available from the start, so they're taken in ascending id order"
      }
    ],
